window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Java 9 → 26 — everything new, version by version", "headings": ["Release timeline", "Jump in", "⚡ Quick start", "📦 Browse by version", "🔬 Deep-dive topics", "Deep-dive topics", "var", "Records", "Sealed classes", "Pattern matching & switch", "Text blocks & Strings", "Modules (JPMS)", "Virtual threads", "Structured concurrency", "Scoped values", "Streams & Gatherers", "Foreign Function & Memory"], "text": "A hands-on reference Java 9 → 26 — everything new, version by version A quick way to learn what changed in Java from 9 to 26 — organised by language features , new APIs , tooling and JVM/other changes, with tiny before → after snippets so you can see the old way and the new way side by side. How the versions work. Java ships every 6 months, but only some releases are LTS (long-term support). This guide has a page for each major stepping-stone release — 9, 11, 17, 21, 25, 26 — and folds the features of the in-between releases into the next stepping stone, always tagged with the version that introduced them (e.g. Added in 14 ). So Java 17 also covers the good stuff from 12–16, Java 21 covers 18–20, and Java 25 covers 22–24. Release timeline Sept 2017 Java 9 Modules (JPMS), JShell, collection factory methods, reactive Flow, HTTP/2 client (incubator). Sept 2018 · LTS Java 11 covers 10 var (from 10), standard HttpClient, run single-file source, new String/Files methods. Sept 2021 · LTS Java 17 covers 12–16 Records, sealed classes, switch expressions, text blocks, pattern matching for instanceof , helpful NPEs. Sept 2023 · LTS Java 21 covers 18–20 Virtual threads, pattern matching for sw var instanceof"}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick start & tooling", "headings": ["1 · Install a JDK", "2 · Install & switch between versions", "SDKMAN! (macOS / Linux / WSL) — recommended", "jenv (per-directory switching)", "Homebrew (macOS)", "Manual — set JAVA_HOME", "3 · JShell — the Java REPL Added in 9", "4 · Run a single source file — no compile step Added in 11", "Multi-file source programs Added in 22", "Shebang scripts", "5 · Try preview language features", "6 · Try incubator modules", "7 · Handy everyday commands", "30-second cheat sheet"], "text": "Set up your machine Quick start & tooling Install one or more JDKs, switch between them, and learn the fastest ways to try features — JShell, single-file programs, preview features and incubator modules. 1 · Install a JDK Pick a build of OpenJDK. Any of these give you the same language; they differ in support/branding: Eclipse Temurin (Adoptium) — the popular community default. Oracle JDK — Oracle's build. Amazon Corretto , Azul Zulu , Microsoft / Liberica — vendor builds, often with extras. Verify what you have: java -version # runtime version javac -version # compiler version java --list-modules | head # JDK modules available 2 · Install & switch between versions You will want several JDKs installed at once (e.g. 17, 21, 25). Use a version manager. SDKMAN! (macOS / Linux / WSL) — recommended curl -s \"https://get.sdkman.io\" | bash # install SDKMAN sdk list java # see available builds sdk install java 25-tem # install Temurin 25 sdk install java 21.0.5-tem sdk use java 25-tem # switch for THIS shell only sdk default java 21.0.5-tem # set the global default jenv (per-directory switching) brew install jenv jenv add /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/temurin-25.jdk/Contents/Home jenv g java -version # runtime version javac -version # compiler version java --list-modules | head # JDK modules available curl -s \"https://get.sdkman.io\" | bash # install SDKMAN sdk list java # see available builds sdk install java 25-tem # install Temurin 25 sdk install java 21.0.5-tem sdk use java 25-tem # switch for THIS shell only sdk default java 21.0.5-tem # set the global default brew install jenv jenv add /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/temurin-25.jdk/Contents/Home jenv global 21 # default jenv local 25 # pin THIS project (writes .java-version) brew install openjdk@25 openjdk@21 openjdk@17 export JAVA_HOME=$(/usr/libexec/java_home -v 25) export PATH=\"$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH\" # Linux: JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/temurin-25 $env:JAVA_HOME = \"C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-25\" $env:Path = \"$env:JAVA"}, {"url": "features.html", "title": "Feature index", "headings": [], "text": "A–Z reference Feature index Every notable change from Java 9 to 26 in one filterable table — what it is, when it landed, whether it's final, and where to read more. Type in the box to filter. Feature Introduced Status Category Learn more Modules (JPMS) 9 Final Language / platform Modules deep-dive Private interface methods 9 Final Language Java 9 Try-with-resources on effectively-final vars 9 Final Language Java 9 var — local-variable type inference 10 Final Language var deep-dive var in lambda parameters 11 Final Language var deep-dive Switch expressions 14 Final Language Pattern matching Text blocks 15 Final Language Text blocks Records 16 Final Language Records deep-dive Pattern matching for instanceof 16 Final Language Pattern matching Sealed classes & interfaces 17 Final Language Sealed classes Pattern matching for switch 21 Final Language Pattern matching Record patterns (destructuring) 21 Final Language Pattern matching Unnamed variables & patterns _ 22 Final Language Pattern matching Flexible constructor bodies 25 Final Language Java 25 Module import declarations 25 Final Language Java 25 Compact source files & instance main 25 Final Language Java 25 Primitive types in patt var var instanceof switch _ main List.of takeWhile dropWhile ofNullable Flow Optional ifPresentOrElse or stream isBlank strip repeat lines Files.readString writeString Collectors.teeing Stream.toList() mapMulti HexFormat jlink java File.java jpackage jwebserver @snippet synchronized"}, {"url": "versions/java-9.html", "title": "Java 9", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Private methods in interfaces", "Try-with-resources on effectively-final variables", "Diamond with anonymous classes · @SafeVarargs on private methods · _ banned", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Immutable collection factory methods", "Stream additions: takeWhile , dropWhile , ofNullable , ranged iterate", "Optional: ifPresentOrElse , or , stream", "Process API — inspect & control OS processes", "Reactive Streams — java.util.concurrent.Flow", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 9 in one glance"], "text": "September 2017 Java 9 The big one: the module system (JPMS) reorganised the JDK itself. Also arrived: JShell , collection factory methods , a reactive Flow API, and many smaller language tidy-ups. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (8 → 9). Strong encapsulation of JDK internals — reflective access to sun.* /internal APIs now warns (and later fails). Run jdeps --jdk-internals your.jar first. Split packages are illegal on the module path — two JARs sharing a package break. _ is no longer a valid identifier. New version string ( 9 , not 1.9 ) — regexes that parsed 1.x break. Applet API and several GC flag combinations deprecated. 1 · Language features Private methods in interfaces Interfaces got default methods in Java 8; now they can share code through private (and private static ) helpers. Before (Java 8) interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } // no way to hide this shared helper default void log(String lvl, String m) { System.out.println(lvl + \": \" + m); } } After (Java 9) interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } private void log(String lvl,  sun.* jdeps --jdk-internals your.jar _ 9 1.9 1.x default private private static interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } // no way to hide this shared helper default void log(String lvl, String m) { System.out.println(lvl + \": \" + m); } } interface Logger { default void info(String m) { log(\"INFO\", m); } default void error(String m) { log(\"ERROR\", m); } private void log(String lvl, String m) { // hidden System.out.println(lvl + \": \" + m); } } // must declare a fresh variable inside the () try (BufferedReader r = reader) { ... } BufferedReader reader = ...; // effectively final try (reader) { // use it directly ... } @SafeVarargs _ Callable<String> c = new Callable<>() { ... }; // diamond now allowed here @SafeVara"}, {"url": "versions/java-11.html", "title": "Java 11 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "var — local-variable type inference Added in 10", "var in lambda parameters Added in 11", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Standard HTTP Client — java.net.http", "New String methods", "Files: read/write a whole string in one call", "Other handy additions", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 11 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2018 · LTS Java 11 LTS The first LTS after 8 and the baseline most teams migrated to. Headliners: var (introduced in 10), the standard HttpClient , running a single source file without compiling, and a batch of convenient String / Files methods. Folded in: features from Java 10 are included here and tagged, since 10 was a non-LTS stepping-stone. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (8 → 11). Java EE & CORBA modules removed — java.xml.bind (JAXB), java.xml.ws (JAX-WS), java.activation , java.corba are gone. Add them as explicit Maven/Gradle dependencies. This is the #1 upgrade break from 8. JavaFX is no longer bundled — use OpenJFX as a separate dependency. Nashorn JavaScript engine deprecated (removed in 15). No more JRE / Server JRE downloads — ship a runtime with jlink . Single JDK: Oracle JDK and OpenJDK are functionally identical (licensing differs). 1 · Language features var — local-variable type inference Added in 10 Before Map<String, List<Integer>> scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Integer>> e : scores.entrySet()) { ... } After (Java 10) var scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (var e : scores.entrySet()) { ... } // type in var String Files java.xml.bind java.xml.ws java.activation java.corba jlink var Map<String, List<Integer>> scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Integer>> e : scores.entrySet()) { ... } var scores = new HashMap<String, List<Integer>>(); for (var e : scores.entrySet()) { ... } // type inferred var var list.forEach((var x) -> System.out.println(x)); map.forEach((@NonNull var k, @NonNull var v) -> ...); // annotations now possible java.net.http HttpURLConnection URL url = new URL(\"https://api.example.com\"); HttpURLConnection c = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection(); c.setRequestMethod(\"GET\"); try (var in = new BufferedReader( new InputStreamReader(c.getInputStream()))) { String body = in.lines().collect(joining(\"\\n\")); } HttpClient client = HttpClient"}, {"url": "versions/java-17.html", "title": "Java 17 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Switch expressions Final in 14", "Records Final in 16", "Sealed classes Final in 17", "Pattern matching for instanceof Final in 16", "Text blocks Final in 15", "Pattern matching for switch Preview in 17", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Stream.toList() Added in 16", "Other API additions", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 17 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2021 · LTS Java 17 LTS A landmark LTS. Over 12–17 the language gained modern data modelling — records , sealed classes , switch expressions , text blocks and pattern matching — turning verbose boilerplate into a few readable lines. Folded in: features from 12 13 14 15 16 are included and tagged with the version that introduced them. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (11 → 17). JDK internals are strongly encapsulated by default (JEP 403) — the old --illegal-access=permit escape hatch is gone. Reflective access to internals now fails; add explicit --add-opens / --add-exports only where needed. Older bytecode-manipulation libs (ASM, cglib, Mockito) may need upgrading. Security Manager deprecated for removal — plan to migrate off it. RMI Activation removed ; experimental AOT/JIT (Graal) compiler removed. Always-strict floating point restored — results may differ slightly from pre-17 non-strictfp math. 1 · Language features Switch expressions Final in 14 switch can return a value, uses arrow labels (no fall-through), and yield for blocks. Before — statement, fall-through, mutable var int days; switch (month) { case JAN: case MAR: case MAY: days = 31; break; case FEB: days = 28; break; de --illegal-access=permit --add-opens --add-exports non-strictfp switch yield int days; switch (month) { case JAN: case MAR: case MAY: days = 31; break; case FEB: days = 28; break; default: days = 30; } int days = switch (month) { case JAN, MAR, MAY -> 31; case FEB -> 28; default -> 30; }; // exhaustive, no break, one assignment public final class Point { private final int x, y; public Point(int x, int y){this.x=x;this.y=y;} public int x(){return x;} public int y(){return y;} public boolean equals(Object o){ /* ... */ } public int hashCode(){ /* ... */ } public String toString(){ /* ... */ } } public record Point(int x, int y) { } // constructor, x()/y(), equals, hashCode, // toString — all generated. public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Rectangle { } public record Circle(d"}, {"url": "versions/java-21.html", "title": "Java 21 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Virtual threads Final in 21", "Pattern matching for switch Final in 21", "Record patterns Final in 21", "Previews that landed here", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Sequenced collections Added in 21", "Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) API Added in 21", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 21 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2023 · LTS Java 21 LTS The concurrency-defining LTS. Virtual threads make blocking code scale to millions of tasks, while pattern matching for switch and record patterns complete Java's data-oriented programming story. Folded in: features from 18 19 20 are included and tagged. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (17 → 21). UTF-8 is the default charset since 18 — code that relied on the platform default (file reads/writes, new String(bytes) ) can change behaviour. Set charsets explicitly. Virtual-thread pinning: on 21–23, blocking inside a synchronized block pins the carrier thread and can starve the pool. Prefer ReentrantLock around blocking I/O (fixed in 24). Dynamic loading of agents warns — attaching agents at runtime is on the way to being disallowed. Finalization deprecated for removal 18 — migrate to Cleaner . String templates that you may have tried in 21 previews were later withdrawn — don't depend on them. 1 · Language features Virtual threads Final in 21 Lightweight threads managed by the JVM. Write simple blocking code; the runtime handles the scale. Before — thread pool, limited var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200); for (var task : tasks) pool.submit(task); // ~200 new String(bytes) synchronized ReentrantLock Cleaner var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200); for (var task : tasks) pool.submit(task); // ~200 concurrent; more = queueing or OOM try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { for (var task : tasks) ex.submit(task); } // millions of concurrent blocking tasks, cheap switch String s; if (obj instanceof Integer i) s = \"int \" + i; else if (obj instanceof String str) s = \"str \" + str; else s = \"other\"; String s = switch (obj) { case Integer i when i > 100 -> \"big int \" + i; case Integer i -> \"int \" + i; case String str -> \"str \" + str; default -> \"other\"; }; instanceof switch if (obj instanceof Point p) { int x = p.x(); int y = p.y(); ... } if (obj instanceof Point(int x, int y)) { ... // x and y bound directly } // nested: s"}, {"url": "versions/java-25.html", "title": "Java 25 LTS", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Compact source files & instance main Final in 25", "Flexible constructor bodies Final in 25", "Module import declarations Final in 25", "Unnamed variables & patterns Final in 22", "Scoped values Final in 25", "Still previewing in 25", "2 · New & updated APIs", "Stream Gatherers Final in 24", "Foreign Function & Memory API Final in 22", "Cryptography", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 25 (LTS) in one glance"], "text": "September 2025 · LTS Java 25 LTS The current LTS. A wave of features graduated to final : scoped values , stream gatherers , the Foreign Function & Memory API , compact source files & instance main , flexible constructor bodies and module import declarations — plus real startup wins from Project Leyden. Folded in: features from 22 23 24 are included and tagged. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (21 → 25). Security Manager permanently disabled 24 — System.setSecurityManager now throws. Any code still using it must be reworked. sun.misc.Unsafe memory-access methods warn and are being removed — migrate to the FFM API / VarHandle . 32-bit x86 port removed — no more 32-bit Linux/Windows JDK builds. Compact object headers change object sizes — tools that assume the old header layout may need updating (opt-in on 24, default-capable on 25). Upside: virtual threads no longer pin on synchronized — safe to drop the 21-era ReentrantLock workarounds. 1 · Language features Compact source files & instance main Final in 25 You can now write a runnable program with no class , no public static , no explicit imports of java.base , and a simple IO helper. The full ceremony is still there when you need it — this  System.setSecurityManager sun.misc.Unsafe VarHandle synchronized ReentrantLock main class public static java.base IO public class Hello { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println(\"Hello, world\"); } } void main() { IO.println(\"Hello, world\"); } // java Hello.java — no class, no static, IO built in super(...) this(...) Range(int lo, int hi) { super(); // must be first if (lo > hi) // validation too late throw new IllegalArgumentException(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } Range(int lo, int hi) { if (lo > hi) // guard BEFORE super() throw new IllegalArgumentException(); super(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } import java.util.List; import java.util.Map; import java.util.stream.Collectors; import java.util.function.Function; import module java.base; // all java.base exports at"}, {"url": "versions/java-26.html", "title": "Java 26", "headings": ["1 · Language features", "Prepare to Make final Mean Final JEP 500", "Primitive types in patterns, instanceof & switch Preview (4th)", "Structured concurrency Preview (6th)", "2 · New & updated APIs", "HTTP/3 for the HTTP Client JEP 517", "Lazy Constants Preview (2nd)", "Also previewing / incubating", "3 · Tooling", "4 · JVM / performance / other", "5 · Summary cheat-sheet", "Java 26 in one glance"], "text": "March 2026 Java 26 A feature release that pushes performance and platform integrity forward: HTTP/3 in the HTTP client, more AOT startup caching , a faster G1 , and the \" final means final \" integrity work — while structured concurrency and primitive patterns edge closer to final. Non-LTS. Java 26 is a 6-month feature release. Production teams on LTS should track these as previews of what's coming ; the next LTS will consolidate them. ⚠ Migration watch-outs (25 → 26). \"final means final\" (JEP 500) — libraries that mutate final fields via setAccessible now warn and will later fail. Watch older serialization, mocking and dependency-injection frameworks. Applet API removed Removed — any lingering applet code will not compile/run. Preparing to restrict JNI — native access continues to tighten; expect to pass --enable-native-access . 1 · Language features Prepare to Make final Mean Final JEP 500 Part of the \"integrity by default\" effort. Historically, libraries could mutate final fields via deep reflection ( setAccessible ). Java 26 begins warning on this so a future release can enforce that final truly is final — enabling constant-folding optimisations and safer code. Before — silently final setAccessible --enable-native-access final final setAccessible final Field f = obj.getClass().getDeclaredField(\"id\"); f.setAccessible(true); f.setInt(obj, 999); // mutates a final field — worked // same code now emits a warning; a future release // will throw. Opt back in per-module only if needed: // --enable-final-field-mutation=<module> instanceof switch Object o = 42; switch (o) { case int i -> System.out.println(\"int \" + i); case double d -> System.out.println(\"double \" + d); default -> {} } // and safe range checks: if (bigValue instanceof int i) { ... } // only matches if it fits in int java.net.http.HttpClient HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder() .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2) .build(); HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder() .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_"}, {"url": "topics/var.html", "title": "var — local-variable type inference Java 10", "headings": ["Why it exists", "Where you can use it", "Where you cannot use it", "Rules & gotchas", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · language var — local-variable type inference Java 10 var lets the compiler infer a local variable's type from its initializer. It is syntactic sugar, not dynamic typing — the variable still has one fixed, static type; you just don't write it out. Why it exists To cut noise where the type is already obvious from the right-hand side, especially with long generic types. It never changes runtime behaviour or performance. Before ByteArrayOutputStream out = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); Map<String, List<Customer>> byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Customer>> e : byCity.entrySet()) { } After var out = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); var byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (var e : byCity.entrySet()) { } Where you can use it var x = 10; // local variable with initializer var list = List.of(\"a\", \"b\"); // inferred List<String> for (var s : list) { } // enhanced-for loop variable for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) { } // classic for-loop index try (var in = Files.newInputStream(p)) { } // try-with-resources list.forEach((var s) -> print(s)); // lambda parameter (Java 11+) Where you cannot use it var x; // ✗ no initializer — nothing t var var ByteArrayOutputStream out = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); Map<String, List<Customer>> byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (Map.Entry<String, List<Customer>> e : byCity.entrySet()) { } var out = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); var byCity = new HashMap<String, List<Customer>>(); for (var e : byCity.entrySet()) { } var x = 10; // local variable with initializer var list = List.of(\"a\", \"b\"); // inferred List<String> for (var s : list) { } // enhanced-for loop variable for (var i = 0; i < 10; i++) { } // classic for-loop index try (var in = Files.newInputStream(p)) { } // try-with-resources list.forEach((var s) -> print(s)); // lambda parameter (Java 11+) var x; // ✗ no initializer — nothing to infer var y = null; // ✗ null has no type var f = () -> 42; // ✗ lambda needs a "}, {"url": "topics/records.html", "title": "Records Final in Java 16", "headings": ["1 · What is a record?", "2 · What the compiler generates", "3 · Customising records", "Compact canonical constructor — validate / normalise", "Extra members that are allowed", "4 · Immutability & defensive copies", "5 · Records + pattern matching", "6 · Records vs class / enum / Lombok", "7 · Serialization", "8 · Pitfalls", "9 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — DTO + multiple return values", "10 · Cheat sheet", "Records in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Records Final in Java 16 A record is a concise, immutable, transparent carrier for a fixed set of values. You declare the components ; the compiler generates the constructor, accessors, equals , hashCode and toString — correctly, every time. 1 line replaces ~40 of boilerplate 5 members auto-generated 100% immutable by default ✓ destructurable with patterns Java 16 final (preview in 14) The problem it solves \"Data classes\" (DTOs, value objects, tuples) were almost all boilerplate — and hand-written equals / hashCode are easy to get subtly wrong (forget a field and you get silent bugs). Records make that boilerplate automatic and correct , and clearly signal intent: this type is just its data . On this page 1. What is a record? 2. What the compiler generates 3. Customising records 4. Immutability & defensive copies 5. Records + pattern matching 6. Records vs class / enum / Lombok 7. Serialization 8. Pitfalls 9. Real-world use cases 10. Cheat sheet 1 · What is a record? You write the shape of the data in the header — the record components — and that's it. The record is implicitly final , its fields are final , and it carries no hidden state beyond record equals hashCode toString equals hashCode final final public final class Point { private final int x, y; public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } public int x() { return x; } public int y() { return y; } @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { if (!(o instanceof Point p)) return false; return x == p.x && y == p.y; } @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); } @Override public String toString() { return \"Point[x=\" + x + \", y=\" + y + \"]\"; } } public record Point(int x, int y) { } enum get equals hashCode toString x() y() equals hashCode toString Point[x=1, y=2] private final java.lang.Record final public Range(int lo, int hi) { if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException(); this.lo = lo; this.hi = hi; } public record Range(int lo, int hi) { pub"}, {"url": "topics/sealed-classes.html", "title": "Sealed classes & interfaces Final in Java 17", "headings": ["1 · What & why", "2 · The three choices each subtype must make", "3 · Rules", "4 · The payoff: exhaustive switch", "5 · Sealed + records = algebraic data types", "6 · Sealed vs enum vs abstract class", "7 · Pitfalls", "8 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — a typed Result instead of exceptions", "9 · Cheat sheet", "Sealed classes in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Sealed classes & interfaces Final in Java 17 A sealed type declares exactly which classes may extend or implement it. That gives you a closed, known set of subtypes — the foundation for exhaustive pattern matching and for modelling \"a value is one of a fixed set of shapes\". closed known subtype set 0 default needed in switch compile-time exhaustiveness check 3 subtype modes: final / sealed / non-sealed Java 17 final (preview in 15) The problem it solves Before, a type was either final (no subtypes) or completely open (anyone, anywhere could subclass). There was no way to say \"these three types and no others\", so the compiler could never know a hierarchy was complete — every switch needed a default and could silently miss a newly added case at runtime. Sealed types close the hierarchy so the compiler can reason about it. On this page 1. What & why 2. final / sealed / non-sealed 3. Rules 4. The payoff: exhaustive switch 5. Sealed + records = ADTs 6. Sealed vs enum vs abstract 7. Pitfalls 8. Real-world use cases 9. Cheat sheet 1 · What & why Legacy — open hierarchy, compiler can't help public abstract class Shape { } // Any code anywhere can do: c sealed default final switch default public abstract class Shape { } // Any code anywhere can do: class Blob extends Shape {} // so every switch over Shape must have a default and // can silently miss new subtypes → runtime surprises. public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Rectangle { } public record Circle(double r) implements Shape { } public record Square(double side) implements Shape { } public record Rectangle(double w, double h) implements Shape { } final Quadrilateral sealed Rectangle non-sealed final sealed permits non-sealed permits sealed interface Expr { } // permits inferred below record Num(int v) implements Expr { } record Add(Expr l, Expr r) implements Expr { } final permits switch default double area(Shape s) { if (s instanceof Circle c) return Math.PI*c.r()*"}, {"url": "topics/pattern-matching-switch.html", "title": "Pattern matching & switch", "headings": ["1 · The family & timeline", "2 · Switch expressions Java 14", "3 · Pattern matching for instanceof Java 16", "4 · Pattern matching for switch + guards Java 21", "5 · Record patterns (destructuring) Java 21", "6 · Unnamed patterns & variables _ Java 22", "7 · Primitive type patterns Preview", "8 · Dominance & exhaustiveness", "9 · Pitfalls", "10 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — render a JSON value", "11 · Cheat sheet", "Pattern matching in one glance"], "text": "Deep dive · language · complete guide Pattern matching & switch A family of related features that let you test a type, bind a variable and destructure data in one step — turning long if / instanceof / cast chains and fall-through switch statements into concise, exhaustive, readable expressions. 3-in-1 test + cast + bind 0 default over sealed/enum nested record destructuring 14→25 built up over releases Java 21 switch patterns final The problem it solves Working with a value whose concrete type varies used to mean a ladder of if (x instanceof T) + cast + extract, or a switch riddled with break and fall-through bugs. Pattern matching makes \"what is this, and pull it apart\" a first-class, compiler-checked operation — the basis of data-oriented programming in Java. On this page 1. The family & timeline 2. Switch expressions 3. instanceof patterns 4. Switch patterns + guards 5. Record patterns 6. Unnamed patterns _ 7. Primitive patterns 8. Dominance & exhaustiveness 9. Pitfalls 10. Real-world use cases 11. Cheat sheet 1 · The family & timeline 14 switch expr 16 instanceof 21 switch patterns + record patterns 22–25 unnamed _ preview primitives Pattern matching arrived in pieces. Each bui if / instanceof / cast switch default if (x instanceof T) switch break switch yield instanceof switch case _ case int i int num; switch (day) { case MON: case TUE: case WED: case THU: case FRI: num = 5; break; // forget break → bug case SAT: case SUN: num = 2; break; default: throw new IllegalStateException(); } int num = switch (day) { case MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI -> 5; case SAT, SUN -> 2; }; // arrow: no fall-through; exhaustive over the enum // block form uses yield: int n = switch (day) { case MON -> { log(day); yield 5; } default -> 0; }; instanceof if (o instanceof String) { String s = (String) o; // repeat the type + cast if (s.length() > 3) use(s); } if (o instanceof String s && s.length() > 3) { use(s); // s bound where the test is true } if (!(o instanceof String s)) return; // "}, {"url": "topics/text-blocks-strings.html", "title": "Text blocks & modern String APIs Java 15", "headings": ["Text blocks", "How indentation works (incidental whitespace)", "Key rules", "Two special escapes for text blocks", "Modern String & text APIs", "Related helpers", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · language + API Text blocks & modern String APIs Java 15 Text blocks are multi-line string literals delimited by \"\"\" — no more \\n , escaped quotes or + concatenation for JSON, SQL, HTML and the like. Paired with a set of String methods added across 9–15. Text blocks Before String html = \"<html>\\n\" + \" <body>\\n\" + \" <p>Hello, \\\"world\\\"</p>\\n\" + \" </body>\\n\" + \"</html>\\n\"; After (Java 15) String html = \"\"\" <html> <body> <p>Hello, \"world\"</p> </body> </html> \"\"\"; How indentation works (incidental whitespace) The compiler finds the line with the least leading whitespace (including the closing \"\"\" ) and strips that much from every line. So you can indent the block to match your code without it appearing in the string. String s = \"\"\" one two \"\"\"; // result: \"one\\n two\\n\" — the common 8-space margin is removed, // the extra 2 spaces before \"two\" are kept. Key rules The opening \"\"\" must be followed by a newline — content starts on the next line. Where the closing \"\"\" sits controls the common indentation and whether there's a trailing newline. No need to escape \" ; escape only \"\"\" if it appears literally. You can still use escapes like \\t , \\n , and \\uXXXX . Two special escapes f \"\"\" \\n + String String html = \"<html>\\n\" + \" <body>\\n\" + \" <p>Hello, \\\"world\\\"</p>\\n\" + \" </body>\\n\" + \"</html>\\n\"; String html = \"\"\" <html> <body> <p>Hello, \"world\"</p> </body> </html> \"\"\"; \"\"\" String s = \"\"\" one two \"\"\"; // result: \"one\\n two\\n\" — the common 8-space margin is removed, // the extra 2 spaces before \"two\" are kept. \"\"\" \"\"\" \" \"\"\" \\t \\n \\uXXXX // \\ (backslash at end of line) — suppress the line break String oneLine = \"\"\" Lorem ipsum dolor \\ sit amet\"\"\"; // \"Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet\" // \\s — a literal space that is NOT stripped as trailing whitespace String padded = \"\"\" key =\\s \"\"\"; // keeps the trailing space after '=' String.formatted(...) formatted String.format isBlank() strip() / stripLeading() / stripTrailing() trim() repeat(n) lines() Stream<String> indent(n) transfo"}, {"url": "topics/modules-jpms.html", "title": "The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) Java 9", "headings": ["Why it exists", "A module in one file: module-info.java", "Directives at a glance", "exports vs. opens — the key distinction", "Classpath vs. module path", "Practical guidance & gotchas", "Related tooling", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · platform The Java Platform Module System (JPMS) Java 9 A module is a named, self-describing group of packages that declares what it needs ( requires ) and what it exposes ( exports ). It adds strong encapsulation and reliable configuration above the old flat classpath. Why it exists Strong encapsulation — packages not exported are truly hidden, even from reflection. Reliable configuration — missing/duplicate/cyclic dependencies are caught at startup, not deep into a run (\"JAR hell\"). A scalable platform — the JDK itself is ~90 modules, so tools like jlink can ship only what you use. A module in one file: module-info.java // src/com.myapp/module-info.java module com.myapp { requires java.net.http; // I depend on this module requires transitive com.mylib; // and anyone who requires me also gets com.mylib exports com.myapp.api; // this package is public API exports com.myapp.internal to com.myapp.tests; // qualified: only to that module uses com.myapp.spi.Plugin; // I consume this service provides com.myapp.spi.Plugin with com.myapp.plugins.DefaultPlugin; // I supply an implementation opens com.myapp.model; // allow deep reflection (e.g. for frameworks) } Directives at a g requires exports exported jlink module-info.java // src/com.myapp/module-info.java module com.myapp { requires java.net.http; // I depend on this module requires transitive com.mylib; // and anyone who requires me also gets com.mylib exports com.myapp.api; // this package is public API exports com.myapp.internal to com.myapp.tests; // qualified: only to that module uses com.myapp.spi.Plugin; // I consume this service provides com.myapp.spi.Plugin with com.myapp.plugins.DefaultPlugin; // I supply an implementation opens com.myapp.model; // allow deep reflection (e.g. for frameworks) } requires M requires transitive M requires static M exports P exports P to M1, M2 opens P opens P to M uses S provides S with Impl ServiceLoader exports com.myapp.api; // Compile & call public types normally. "}, {"url": "topics/virtual-threads.html", "title": "Virtual threads Final in Java 21", "headings": ["1 · What is a virtual thread?", "2 · Platform vs virtual threads", "3 · Architecture & how it works", "4 · Creating virtual threads", "5 · Executors & task fan-out", "6 · Lifecycle & mount/unmount", "7 · Pinning (and the JDK 24 fix)", "8 · Relationship with structured concurrency", "9 · Relationship with scoped values", "10 · Virtual threads in Spring Boot", "11 · Virtual threads vs WebFlux / reactive", "12 · Pitfalls & anti-patterns", "13 · Monitoring & debugging", "14 · Migration guide", "15 · Real-world use cases", "Worked example — check 10,000 URLs concurrently", "16 · Cheat sheet", "Virtual threads in one glance", "Evolution"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency · complete guide Virtual threads Final in Java 21 Virtual threads are extremely cheap threads managed by the JVM instead of the operating system. They let you keep writing simple, blocking, one-thread-per-task code while scaling to millions of concurrent tasks — the performance of async, with the simplicity of plain threads. 1M+ concurrent virtual threads < 1 KB memory per thread (vs ~1 MB) < 1 µs to create (sub-microsecond) 0 OS threads wasted while blocked 100% plain blocking code (no rewrite) The problem it solves Servers used to face a lose-lose choice: thread-per-request code was simple but capped at a few thousand OS threads, while reactive/async code scaled but was hard to write and debug. Virtual threads end the trade-off — you write ordinary blocking, thread-per-request code and it still scales to hundreds of thousands of concurrent requests, because blocking a virtual thread no longer wastes an OS thread. On this page 1. What is a virtual thread? 2. Platform vs virtual threads 3. Architecture & how it works 4. Creating virtual threads 5. Executors & task fan-out 6. Lifecycle & mount/unmount 7. Pinning (and the JDK 24 fix) 8. With structured concurr // You size a pool and guard it, because OS // threads are scarce and blocking wastes them. ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200); for (Request r : requests) { pool.submit(() -> { var user = userService.load(r.userId()); // blocks! var orders = orderService.list(r.userId()); // blocks! return render(user, orders); }); } // > 200 in-flight requests → they queue and latency spikes. // One virtual thread per request. Blocking is fine: // the carrier is freed while this VT waits. try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { for (Request r : requests) { exec.submit(() -> { var user = userService.load(r.userId()); // blocks cheaply var orders = orderService.list(r.userId()); // blocks cheaply return render(user, orders); }); } } // 100k+ concurrent requests,"}, {"url": "topics/structured-concurrency.html", "title": "Structured concurrency Preview", "headings": ["The problem: unstructured concurrency leaks", "Why it's better", "Joiners — choosing the completion policy (Java 25)", "Deadlines & cancellation", "Gotchas", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency Structured concurrency Preview Structured concurrency treats a group of related subtasks as a single unit of work . They start together, and the scope guarantees they all finish (or are cancelled) before the block exits — no leaked threads, clear error propagation, honest stack traces. Still a preview feature (5th preview in Java 25, 6th in 26). Compile/run with --enable-preview . The API shown here is the reworked Java 25 shape; earlier releases used ShutdownOnFailure / ShutdownOnSuccess (see Evolution). The problem: unstructured concurrency leaks Before — ExecutorService, manual coordination Future<User> u = pool.submit(() -> findUser(id)); Future<Order> o = pool.submit(() -> fetchOrder(id)); try { return new Response(u.get(), o.get()); // if findUser throws, fetchOrder keeps running (leak); // cancellation & error handling are all manual and easy to botch } catch (Exception e) { u.cancel(true); o.cancel(true); // did you remember both? throw e; } After — one scope, one unit try (var scope = StructuredTaskScope.open()) { // Java 25 API Subtask<User> u = scope.fork(() -> findUser(id)); Subtask<Order> o = scope.fork(() -> fetchOrder(id)); scope.join(); // wa --enable-preview ShutdownOnFailure ShutdownOnSuccess Future<User> u = pool.submit(() -> findUser(id)); Future<Order> o = pool.submit(() -> fetchOrder(id)); try { return new Response(u.get(), o.get()); // if findUser throws, fetchOrder keeps running (leak); // cancellation & error handling are all manual and easy to botch } catch (Exception e) { u.cancel(true); o.cancel(true); // did you remember both? throw e; } try (var scope = StructuredTaskScope.open()) { // Java 25 API Subtask<User> u = scope.fork(() -> findUser(id)); Subtask<Order> o = scope.fork(() -> fetchOrder(id)); scope.join(); // wait for all; propagate the first failure, // cancelling the siblings automatically return new Response(u.get(), o.get()); } // on exit, every subtask is guaranteed done join() // Fail fast: all must s"}, {"url": "topics/scoped-values.html", "title": "Scoped values Java 25", "headings": ["Why not just ThreadLocal?", "Before & after", "Reading the value", "Binding: run vs call , and multiple values", "Works across subtasks with structured concurrency", "Gotchas", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · concurrency Scoped values Java 25 A ScopedValue shares immutable data with methods further down the call stack without passing it as a parameter — a modern, safer, virtual-thread-friendly replacement for many uses of ThreadLocal . Why not just ThreadLocal? ThreadLocal ScopedValue Mutability Mutable any time ( set ) Immutable; bound for a scope only Lifetime Until removed (leaks if you forget remove() ) Auto-unbound when the scope block exits Inheritance Copied to child threads (expensive) Shared by structure, no copying At scale Costly with millions of virtual threads Designed for it Before & after Before — ThreadLocal, mutable, leak-prone static final ThreadLocal<User> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>(); void handle(Request r) { CURRENT.set(authenticate(r)); try { process(); // deep code calls CURRENT.get() } finally { CURRENT.remove(); // MUST remember, or you leak / see stale data } } After — ScopedValue, immutable, self-cleaning static final ScopedValue<User> CURRENT = ScopedValue.newInstance(); void handle(Request r) { ScopedValue.where(CURRENT, authenticate(r)) .run(() -> process()); // bound only inside run(...) } // automatically unbound here — nothing to clean up Re ScopedValue ThreadLocal set remove() static final ThreadLocal<User> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>(); void handle(Request r) { CURRENT.set(authenticate(r)); try { process(); // deep code calls CURRENT.get() } finally { CURRENT.remove(); // MUST remember, or you leak / see stale data } } static final ScopedValue<User> CURRENT = ScopedValue.newInstance(); void handle(Request r) { ScopedValue.where(CURRENT, authenticate(r)) .run(() -> process()); // bound only inside run(...) } // automatically unbound here — nothing to clean up void process() { User u = CURRENT.get(); // whatever was bound in the enclosing scope if (CURRENT.isBound()) { ... } // check before get() to avoid an exception } run call // run: no result; call: returns a value / may throw checked exceptions String out = ScopedValue.w"}, {"url": "topics/streams-gatherers.html", "title": "Stream Gatherers Java 24", "headings": ["The gap it fills", "Built-in gatherers", "Before & after — sliding windows", "Writing your own gatherer", "Other recent Stream additions worth knowing", "Gotchas", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · API Stream Gatherers Java 24 A Gatherer is a custom intermediate stream operation — the long-missing counterpart to a custom Collector . Where collect ends a stream, gather transforms it mid-pipeline: windowing, running totals, dedup-by-key, throttling and more. The gap it fills Streams shipped with a fixed set of intermediate ops ( map , filter , limit …). Anything stateful or many-to-one across elements — sliding windows, \"stop after N distinct\", running averages — meant dropping out of the stream. Gatherers make those first-class and composable. Built-in gatherers import java.util.stream.Gatherers; // fixed-size batches Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5).gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[3,4],[5]] // sliding windows Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.windowSliding(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]] // running fold (like reduce but emits each step) Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.scan(() -> 0, Integer::sum)).toList(); // [1,3,6,10] // stateful fold to a single value, emitted at the end Stream.of(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\").gather(Gatherers.fold(() -> \"\", String::concat)).toList(); // [\"abc\"] // bounded-parallelism mapping that preserves order urls.stream().gather(Gather Gatherer Collector collect gather map filter limit import java.util.stream.Gatherers; // fixed-size batches Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5).gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[3,4],[5]] // sliding windows Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.windowSliding(2)).toList(); // [[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]] // running fold (like reduce but emits each step) Stream.of(1,2,3,4).gather(Gatherers.scan(() -> 0, Integer::sum)).toList(); // [1,3,6,10] // stateful fold to a single value, emitted at the end Stream.of(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\").gather(Gatherers.fold(() -> \"\", String::concat)).toList(); // [\"abc\"] // bounded-parallelism mapping that preserves order urls.stream().gather(Gatherers.mapConcurrent(10, this::fetch)).toList(); List<List<Integer>> windows = new ArrayList<>(); for (int i = 0; i + 3 <= xs.size(); i++"}, {"url": "topics/ffm-api.html", "title": "Foreign Function & Memory API Java 22", "headings": ["Why it replaces JNI", "Two halves of the API", "1 · Off-heap memory: Arena & MemorySegment", "2 · Calling native functions: Linker & method handles", "Upcalls — pass a Java method as a C callback", "jextract — generate bindings automatically", "Gotchas", "Evolution", "Cheat card"], "text": "Deep dive · API Foreign Function & Memory API Java 22 The FFM API lets pure Java call native (C) functions and access off-heap memory safely and without writing any C glue. It's the modern, supported replacement for JNI and the old sun.misc.Unsafe tricks. Why it replaces JNI JNI (old) FFM (Java 22+) Native glue Hand-written C, compiled per platform None — pure Java Safety Easy to crash the JVM Bounded, typed, lifetime-checked memory Memory Unsafe , manual, leak-prone Arena with deterministic release Ergonomics Boilerplate-heavy Fluent method handles + layouts Two halves of the API 1 · Off-heap memory: Arena & MemorySegment Before — ByteBuffer / Unsafe ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(16) .order(ByteOrder.nativeOrder()); buf.putInt(0, 42); int v = buf.getInt(0); // no explicit lifetime; freeing is up to the GC/cleaner After — Arena + MemorySegment try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) { MemorySegment seg = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 4); seg.setAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0, 42); int v = seg.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0); } // memory released deterministically here Arena controls lifetime & access: ofConfined() (one thread, cheapest), ofShared() (many thr sun.misc.Unsafe Unsafe Arena Arena MemorySegment ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(16) .order(ByteOrder.nativeOrder()); buf.putInt(0, 42); int v = buf.getInt(0); // no explicit lifetime; freeing is up to the GC/cleaner try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) { MemorySegment seg = arena.allocate(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 4); seg.setAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0, 42); int v = seg.getAtIndex(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0); } // memory released deterministically here ofConfined() ofShared() ofAuto() global() Linker // call C's size_t strlen(const char *s); Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker(); SymbolLookup std = linker.defaultLookup(); MethodHandle strlen = linker.downcallHandle( std.find(\"strlen\").orElseThrow(), FunctionDescriptor.of(ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG, // return: size_t ValueLayout.ADDR"}];
